<section class="owners" id="owners">
  <div class="owners-bg-strip"></div>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="owners-inner">
      <div class="owners-text">
        <p class="section-label">Original Owners</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Sherman &amp; Elizabeth Booth</h2>
        <div class="divider"></div>

        <h3 class="owners-subhead">A Family of Conviction</h3>

        <p>
          Sherman Miller Booth II was born in Philadelphia in 1876 — the year of the American
          Centennial — and grew up in Wisconsin, the son of the abolitionist Sherman Miller Booth I,
          whose defiance of the Fugitive Slave Act in 1854 became one of the defining legal dramas
          of the pre-Civil War era. The elder Booth was editor of the Milwaukee abolitionist
          newspaper <em>The Free Democrat</em>. When the escaped slave Joshua Glover was captured
          by federal marshals in Racine and held under the hated Fugitive Slave Act, Sherman Booth I
          rode on horseback to rally a crowd of nearly 5,000 people in the courthouse square. The
          mob broke through the jail door and Glover escaped to freedom in Canada. The case wound
          through the courts as <em>Ableman v. Booth</em>, ultimately reaching the United States
          Supreme Court in 1859, where it became a landmark ruling on the balance between state and
          federal power. The younger Sherman grew up in the long shadow of that legacy.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sherman II studied law and established his practice in Chicago at 105 S. LaSalle Street.
          In 1907, he married Elizabeth Crutchfield Knox, born October 26, 1880, in Cedar Rapids,
          Iowa. She had attended Coe College and taught English in Osage, Iowa, before her marriage.
          The couple settled in Glencoe and had three children: Knox (born 1908), Sherman III (born
          1910), and Betty (born 1917).
        </p>

        <h3 class="owners-subhead">Elizabeth and the Vote</h3>

        <p>
          Elizabeth Knox Booth became one of the most consequential figures in the Illinois
          suffrage movement. In 1912 she organized and served as the first president of the Glencoe
          Equal Suffrage Association, then moved to the state level as Chairman of the Legislative
          Department of the Illinois Equal Suffrage Association. Her method was meticulous rather
          than theatrical: she implemented an intricate index-card system that catalogued every
          member of the state House and Senate, tracking their voting history and suffrage pledges
          so the campaign could approach each legislator with precision.
        </p>

        <p>
          The approach worked. On June 26, 1913, Elizabeth was present in Springfield when
          Governor Edward F. Dunne signed the Illinois women's suffrage bill into law. With that
          signature, Illinois became the first state east of the Mississippi River to grant women
          the right to vote for president of the United States — seven years before the Nineteenth
          Amendment made suffrage universal. Elizabeth's work with Grace Wilbur Trout, Antoinette
          Funk, and Margaret Hayley had made it possible.
        </p>

        <h3 class="owners-subhead">The Wright Friendship</h3>

        <p>
          The Booths' relationship with Frank Lloyd Wright grew from a professional engagement into
          a genuine friendship. Both Sherman and Wright had grown up in Wisconsin; both wives were
          educators. The professional bond was forged under pressure: in December 1911, newspapers
          were fixated on Wright's affair with Mamah Borthwick, and Sherman Booth publicly
          represented both Wright and his estranged wife Catherine in the controversy. Booth also
          handled the American distribution of the Wasmuth Portfolio — the landmark two-volume
          folio of 100 lithographs of Wright's work published in Berlin in 1910–11 by the
          publisher Ernst Wasmuth — navigating litigation around its publication and distribution
          in the United States. Discussions about the Glencoe commission appear in correspondence
          as early as January 1910, written while Wright was aboard the R.M.S. Lusitania en route
          to Germany to oversee the portfolio's production.
        </p>


        <div class="owners-details">
